    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This study proposes a rigorous opti-mization of lipid nanoparticle (LNP) architecture through the strategic substitution of standard ionizable lipids with novel branched-tail amphiphiles, directly correlating increased unsaturation and molecular conformational entropy with a measurable elevation in the lipid bilayer's phase transition temperature (Tm). This thermodynamic stiffening of the phospholipid matrix fundamentally decelerates the kinetic rate of mRNA strand hydrolysis by reducing the local free volume available for nucleophile attack and simultaneously acts as a potent kinetic barrier against the premature desolvation and release of the encapsulated genomic payload during sub-ambient 4°C refrigeration cycles. Furthermore, the co-incorporation of highly saturated, sterically hindered cholesterol derivatives within the hydrophobic interior induces a reduction in translational entropy and increases interfacial viscosity, creating a more robust disorder-to-order phase transition that mitigates off-shell leakage and shields stromal nucleic acids from reactive oxygen species-induced oxidative damage. These sophisticated physicochemical modifications collectively enhance the colloidal stability and long-term encapsulation efficiency of the mRNA therapeutic candidate, establishing a critical blueprint for manufacturing solvent- and temperature-free shelf-stable medical products without compromising the immunogenic",
      "source": "target",
